Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(movement): retain completed client prediction frames

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 packets distinguish completed fluid motion
from air gravity. At eye Y 103.4815826 a native idle-water frame reports
-0.022315647 blocks per tick, while the previous air approximation reports
-0.0996117 for the matching translated position. Preserve a negotiated
client motion sample instead of reconstructing it from rounded positions.

Consume each sample once at CLIENT_TICK_END, verify that its feet position
matches the standard Java movement stream, and reject non-finite values,
unknown fields and incompatible protocol revisions. Server state and
vehicle prediction remain authoritative; ordinary Java clients retain
their existing approximation. Preserve the sampled floor motion: native
air-floor gameplay reports -0.0784, while water-floor motion is -0.005.

Track local swimming transitions from the same completed frame, including
ViaProxy clients. The client integration sends these frames after normal
Java movement and before the tick-end packet. Keep correction handling,
auth-input construction, frame validation and event state in core.

Carry completed horizontal and vertical collision axes with every prediction
frame, including stationary frames that Java omits from position updates.
Consume them only for a matching unmounted frame outside dimension changes.
Native 1.26.51.1 wall and open-water captures distinguish collision from
grounded state and clear horizontal collision on the release frame.

Apply corrected player velocity in all poses and retain zero-velocity resets.
Java relative velocity flags previously discarded this authority outside
gliding. Connections without the negotiated physical correction channel apply
these values directly. Reserve tick zero as the target receiver does.
Full native history admission and input resimulation remain required.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 pool-floor jumping holds Jumping, JumpDown
and WantUp without StartJumping. The old grounded-plus-held heuristic
sends a false StartJumping on the first water-rise frame even though
the full 29-frame position and motion sequence already matches native.

Accept the actual ground-jump impulse in completed prediction frames and
use it for StartJumping. Do not infer this event from motion thresholds,
held input or grounded state when the client supplies local physics.
Use a separately negotiated revision-five channel to prevent earlier
clients from sending an incompatible layout. Keep the standard Java
approximation for connections without a completed local frame.

Evaluate the native SneakTriggerIntent posture rules in core. The pinned
1.26.51.1 executable separates swimming, crawling and sneaking flags, probes
standing/crouching/crawling block space, and uses a 0.49999997F movement
threshold when crouching space is clear. Low ceilings switch swimming to
crawling outside body water and crawling back to swimming on water entry.
Keep these decisions independent from delayed server metadata.

Revision five transports all three completed posture flags. Emit swimming
and crawling edges once per connection, and distinguish effective sneaking
from physical SneakDown/WantDown input while preserving shield blocking.
The add-on supplies collision probes and local input; core owns the rule,
wire validation and Bedrock events. Metadata history rewind and input
resimulation remain incomplete.

Bypass posture input slowdown during flight, previous swimming, or water
contact for this frame. Matching 1.26.51.1 calculator 0x1404495e0 and its
adapter 0x14669d7d0 identify WasInWaterFlagComponent as the water bypass.
The movement request writer 0x148b58fe0 copies the processed coordinates.
Carry pre-input water contact in revision 5 so core packet construction
and client physics share the rule, including the crawl-to-water frame.
Reject incompatible revisions and exercise all 128 flag combinations.

Native SneakingSystem 0x14b192c50 and callback 0x14b193600 compute
min(1F, 0.3F + equipped Swift Sneak level * 0.15F) with separate float
operations. Equipment update 0x1432760e0 reads enchantment 37 from leg
slot 2, and lookup 0x1432602c0 returns the first matching enchantment.
Use this scalar for auth input instead of fixed 0.3, including ordinary
Java clients. Read only typed Bedrock enchantment entries and preserve
water, previous-swimming and flight bypasses. The add-on shares the
same scalar for local input physics. Item-use scaling remains separate.

The native scalar producer grants the enchantment bonus only when the
prior SNEAKING or CRAWLING flag is set. The initial physical sneak press
therefore uses 0.3 before the posture transition. Apply the same previous
posture in core and local physics. Keep normalized diagonal input instead
of Java's square-speed expansion, which strict BDS rejects while sneaking.
The native input calculator normalizes before applying the sneak scalar.

Equipment update 0x1432760e0 creates SwiftSneakEnchantComponent only when
absent and removes it for nonpositive enchantment levels. Positive-to-
positive replacement retains the original component level. Share this
lifecycle in SwiftSneakState and advance equipment state even while idle.
Strict BDS produces the same retained-level behavior; do not fit physics
to a newly read level that the pinned native component has not adopted.

Native parser 0x14519b430 reads the low byte of a short enchantment ID,
defaults incorrectly typed IDs/levels to zero, and preserves entry order.
Lookup 0x1432602c0 takes the first matching entry. Share that level lookup
with valid Java enchantment translation so duplicate entries cannot give
local physics a different Swift Sneak level from the core input vector.

Retain SneakingComponent as state rather than deriving this frame's scalar
from the immediately preceding pose. Registration 0x14681da00 places
ClientInputUpdateSystem before SneakingSystem and SneakTriggerActionSystem
later in the pipeline. Consume the retained scale, produce its successor
from the previous flags, then apply the completed posture. This preserves
the initial input phase and component lifecycle without tick counters.

Exercise the shared direction helper across all sixteen cardinal-key
combinations and six native scalar values. Verify zero movement when both
axes cancel, cardinal movement when one axis cancels, and normalized
movement before the scalar is applied. Keep unrelated held flags intact.

Match native ItemUseSlowdownSystem and ItemUseSlowdownApplySystem in the
pinned 1.26.51.1 executable. The reader at 0x143fd7e10 defaults an absent
component to .35, but a present component without a modifier to 1F.
The producer at 0x14c5f5e00 suppresses the component at FLT_EPSILON from 1.
The consumer at 0x14c60f9a0 squares that modifier before multiplying each
posture-scaled direction and clears the gameplay input cache. The phase-9
reset at 0x14c610370 removes the component after each frame.

Translate native item use modifiers and vibrations into Java USE_EFFECTS
in core, including network and resource-pack definitions. Keep shield
posture slowdown separate. Revision 6 carries the actual completed item
use scale so consumption, release, or slot changes cannot reconstruct a
different scale from the new inventory state. Standard Java clients use
server USINGITEM state as a fallback. Keep native scalar calculations and
packet construction in core; the add-on only samples the translated item
component and applies it after posture input.

Port unboosted glide travel into the shared core GlideMovement calculator.
Matching 1.26.51.1 GlideMoveSystem callback 0x146667b10 reconstructs view
angles with the float lookup, applies lift and pitch exchange with separate
float operations, and damps motion before collision resolution. Slow
Falling selects gravity for this kernel even during ascent. Preserve the
native multiplication order; reassociation changes rounded velocity.

Exact native instructions and the Java calculator agree in all 4,366
controlled cases, including angle seams, vertical views, ascending and
falling motion, Slow Falling, and randomized rotations. Fixtures supply
CRT remainder, the regenerated sine table, status slots and an absent
rocket boost. Retain compact numeric regression cases in Java tests.
The add-on invokes this calculation for local Bedrock glide travel on
direct and ViaProxy sessions. Collision solving, rocket effects, full
history replay, and live flight comparisons remain separate requirements.

Decode protocol 2193 MovementEffect into retained per-actor state and a
versioned client payload. Preserve signed duration, unsigned server tick
bits, and the exact binding to an accepted completed Java frame. Advance
prediction transport to revision 7 and retain 512 tick/frame bindings.
Missing or evicted bindings never restart a boost from receipt time.
Snapshots retain original timing for late channel registration.

Port the native confirmed glide boost before drag. Callback 0x146667b10
sums the complete impulse before adding entering motion; Java and the
native function match all 8,732 controlled boosted/unboosted cases.
Target 1.26.51.1 schema and packet vtable 0x14e862850 establish packet ID
318 and field offsets. Native helper 0x142fe8990 and tick callback
0x1490daf80 match 216 admission/countdown trials without history.

The add-on consumes the shared clock and calculation on both connection
routes. Full prediction replay, speculative rocket admission, input phases,
dolphin/geyser physics, ordinary Java fallback, and live joins remain open.
No matching native client trajectory was executed in this boot.

Retain and transport authoritative minecraft:underwater_movement updates
through a protocol-versioned channel after PLAY and spawn, including late
registration. Preserve clamping and explicit zero; never invent a speed
when the server supplied no attribute. Strict BDS 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193
sends player speed .02 in its initial attributes.

Share confirmed dolphin boost acceleration and damping with local physics.
Native DolphinBoostSystem callback 0x148b99e40 admits the multiplier only
for SWIMMING with an active slot-one effect. Water speed 0x14207c030 keeps
full Depth Strider while boosted, including airborne motion. Damping at
0x142dee630 bypasses Depth Strider drag interpolation for this multiplier.
The production Java kernels match all 6,992 native speed/damping cases;
2,048 native admission cases also pass. Fixtures supply ECS storage,
attributes, enchantment maximum and plain-player traits.

Keep the shared confirmation clock. Native packet application, correction
history, geyser physics, unknown attribute defaults and custom water drag
traits remain incomplete. The isolated strict-BDS synthetic probe confirms
the attribute but emits no boost, so it does not prove client trajectory
parity. Live native/direct/ViaProxy boost comparisons remain required.

Generalize the dolphin kernel into WaterMovement for ordinary and boosted
water travel. Native getter 0x14207c030 multiplies the walking/underwater
speed difference by the clamped Depth Strider level before division by
three. Java instead multiplies by a precomputed fraction, which changes
float rounding. Airborne ordinary travel halves the enchantment level;
boosted travel retains full efficiency.

Apply ordinary drag interpolation in shared float arithmetic at native
callback 0x142dee630. Keep sprint drag, absent water-trait default .8 at
the observation boundary, and boost bypass behavior. Java production and
native instructions agree in all 19,456 bounded speed/damping cases.
Fixtures supply ECS storage, attribute/enchantment observations and the
walking-speed virtual boundary. The add-on supplies local speed, equipment,
ground and water-trait observations on direct and ViaProxy sessions.
Native phase ordering, custom trait mapping, input/collision solving,
complete fluid gravity, currents and history replay remain requirements.

Match native LiquidPhysicsSystem 0x1495e44e0 in the pinned executable.
Accumulate observed cell flows in float, preserve the inner Y/X square sum,
normalize at length .0001F, and add the float impulse to entering motion.
Water uses .014F and lava .0035F; Java retains a different lava strength.
The full native callback passes 1,962 controlled executions, including
policy gates, cancellation, tiny currents, and water/lava selection.
The production Java arithmetic matches all 654 admitted cases exactly.
Fixtures supply loaded-world/contact observations and per-cell flow vectors;
flow generation, iteration order, contact policy and scheduling remain open.
Share this arithmetic with client integration on both connection routes.

Port the native separated-axis contact output into core. Matching
1.26.51.1 kernel 0x143327d80 rounds box distances in float32 and
snaps their absolute value at 0.000001F to positive zero. Java
voxel traversal skips a wall cell after a rounded teleport crosses
its face, allowing repeated current-driven penetration and strict
BDS corrections. Share the actual contact rule with client queries.
Keep actor depenetration, Y/X/Z sequencing, native shape order, and
step finalization as separate incomplete solver requirements.

Retain authoritative grounded state alongside every player correction.
Java player-position packets cannot encode it, and even a zero-relative
entity teleport starts two-step local interpolation in Java 26.3. Send a
negotiated correction payload before the standard position packet and
pair it by player and teleport ID. Core retains the native correction
tick and owns the codec and one-use pairing lifecycle. Ordinary clients
keep standard position and velocity translation. In-history rewind and
resimulation remain separate requirements.

Refresh the glide regression values after correcting the shared native
sine table initializer. Execute 8,732 boosted and unboosted target kernel
cases with the corrected producer table and compare every float output.
These cases retain supplied sinf, CRT remainder, status slots and boost
components; they do not close the separate Windows CRT rounding gap.

Implement confirmed geyser boost calculations from native MovementTick
0x146655f00 and its original block-column helpers. Share the 24-cell
downward scan, both block layers, source-water depth, clear-top checks,
flying exclusion, strict profile height and pre-addition velocity caps.
Preserve float rounding and cap overshoot. Keep world access separate
from the core rule so client integrations can use local collision shapes.

Private execution of the pinned 1.26.51.1 callback and all four helpers
matches 6,780 positive and negative cases. Original property lookup and
component admission execute unchanged. Native world trajectory, client
phase ordering and correction/history comparisons remain required.

Reproduce local sulfur ticker admission from the pinned native block
entity producer 144ae5140. Use strict actor AABB overlap with the native
water-depth range, then refresh the 100-frame geyser effect without
shortening longer or infinite active effects. Keep admission separate
from the center-column lift query. Tick-zero server confirmations are
not sufficient to reproduce native local effect prediction.

Native incoming handler 14133f140 uses direct application without replay
state, or duration adjustment and history commands with replay state.
Do not invent a receipt-time deadline for an unknown input tick. Complete
confirmation command replay and native phase ordering remain required.

Match native movement-effect command ordering for known client frames.
The pinned constructor and live command callback use an active-state
transition to force application, then clear the command ordering stamp.
Keep the local prediction frame separately from the packet wire tick.
Retain core snapshots in arrival order and allow infinite effects without
an expiration clock binding. Do not infer a finite deadline for an unknown
frame; full raw input-clock transport and history replay remain required.

Execute 16,038 native constructor/application cases, 3,521 dispatcher
cases and 6,144 combined command/ring cases privately against build
1.26.51.1. A native countdown sequence reproduces three old-state failures;
targeted production tests now match removal, activation and infinite-state
results. The fixture does not execute complete world motion or rewind.

Native command constructor 0x143281540 uses the replay input tick even
when the packet tick has no retained frame. Preserve the latest emitted
input tick and paired Java frame, and invalidate the pair on an input
without a matching prediction. Send both through a separately negotiated
revision-two movement-effect channel; earlier clients never receive the
new layout. Keep exact history identity independent from this clock.

For past confirmations with a usable clock, reproduce unsigned tick
comparison and signed 32-bit duration adjustment, then count down from
the paired frame. This includes tick-zero startup effects and long effects
whose source frame was evicted. Repeated snapshots do not renew duration,
and local geyser admission preserves a longer adjusted remainder.
The pinned 1.26.51.1 executable matched all 16,038 constructor/application
probe cases. Future unbound commands, replay controller scheduling,
actor replay-component creation and full physics rewind remain gaps.

Original 1.26.51.1 command construction and live application retain the
full duration for future ticks instead of delaying their activation.
Use every paired input clock for duration snapshots, including future
commands without an exact history frame. A future command can supersede
local prediction when its clock reference is at or after that prediction.
Keep unpaired clocks unknown rather than infer raw input ticks from frames.

A combined native fixture executes 420 constructor/dispatch/replay cases,
including real queued movement-effect callbacks and the original countdown.
It exposes 24 sampled live activity mismatches before this timing fix.
Retain the original snapshot anchor when a confirmed effect is resent.
Original-duration restoration during history rewind and pending-command
drain still require complete production history and physics replay.

Native client registration installs 367 systems and includes 215 in its
correction movement category. GeyserBoostSystem::MovementTick precedes
MovementEffectsTick. Original registered dispatch, effect admission, lift
and countdown execute in 32 bounded cases across 160 steps. A one-tick
confirmed effect performs one lift before expiration; querying the next
frame incorrectly skipped it. Age server confirmations at the completed
frame while preserving next-step timestamps for local effect admission.
Compare forced transitions and longer-effect preservation in the same
phase. Retained snapshots keep their original clock and cannot renew time.
Full packet-phase handling of zero-duration markers and complete world
rewind remain separate verification requirements.

Completed local frame IDs increase throughout a connection. Retain the last accepted identity after consumption or position rejection so duplicate and older payloads cannot bind stale motion and posture to a later auth-input tick. Keep rider time bindings unchanged. Targeted storage and real custom-payload handler regressions verify frame order, rejected positions, skipped frames and connection reset.

Retain independent flight speed, raw inherited game mode, legacy friction
flag127 and native drag/friction modifiers. Refresh the negotiated versioned
client snapshot after join and authoritative mode, ability, metadata and
attribute changes. Keep calculation and state in core so the add-on uses
the same rules through direct connections and ViaProxy.

Matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 original control and friction
routines match 18,000 vertical and 3,456 drag/friction cases bit for bit.
Preserve independent normal and slow requests and native float operation
order, idle creative coasting, spectator retention and motion cutoff.
Targeted stateful tests exercise raw packet serialization and lifecycle
with scoped mocks. Their explicit test-only Mockito agent avoids dynamic
agent loading and global protocol or mapping mutations.

Full collision phases, trajectories, strict BDS, Boar and historical
reconciliation remain required; bounded native calculations do not close
those verification gates.

Reproduce target 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 SwimControl interpolation with
separate float subtraction, multiplication and addition. Flying uses 1.3F;
ordinary control uses 0.085F below -0.2F and 0.06F otherwise. Keep independent
slow descent distinct from normal WantDown, and expose native physical jump
eligibility without clearing the raw vertical input requests.

Original startup 146822800/nested 14681da00 registers VerticalFlySpeedControl,
JumpInput and SwimControl in that order. Original manager registration and
category executor preserve it; 1425c6a00 is another registration overload, not
a sorting pass. Original callback 1490c0a00 matches 3072 supplied input cases.
A stateful original callback sequence covers flight start/stop, held jump,
simultaneous vertical requests, swimming/material and WasInWater changes.

Native angle-table initialization, complete world collision and live strict
BDS/direct/ViaProxy trajectories remain separate verification requirements.

Preserve the SwimControl pitch producer through wrapped current-minus-previous
float degrees, negative float radians and the shared Windows sine lookup.
The actual 1.26.51.1 callback 1490c0a00 with the separately verified official
Windows table matches every look and motion bit across 26,660 supplied cases.
Java 26.3 Mth differs at index and table rounding boundaries. Keep this helper
in core and its client binding limited to negotiated local swimming flight.
Rotation component construction, scheduler and breathing material producers
remain separate native boundaries. These checks do not prove live trajectories.

Pair admitted completed-frame position and velocity in auth input. The
original 1.26.51.1 sender copies current StateVector XYZ into mPos and
velocity XYZ into mPosDelta. Java 26.3 suppresses position sends below
0.0002 blocks, so retaining the last Java move coordinates alongside a
fresh completed velocity produces a stale position even on idle/reversal
frames. Apply matching completed coordinates before advancing tick
history, through the same spawn, teleport and chunk admission gates.
Keep the existing feet/origin float conversion and ordinary Java fallback.

Discard unconsumed physics on authoritative position changes, including
corrections smaller than the matching tolerance. Preserve the connection
frame watermark and emitted input-tick history so rejected or consumed
frames cannot rebind and prior correction clocks remain usable. Mounted
and dimension-change frames retain their existing clock bindings without
applying player coordinates.

Actual packet regressions cover reversal, missing/replayed/mismatched
frames, unloaded boundaries, signed correction acknowledgement, server
correction, mounting, mode changes, lifecycle and float conversion. Two
unchanged-source negative controls reproduce stale coordinates and stale
pre-correction physics. Original sender instruction fixtures establish
field binding under supplied current-state inputs; full native trajectories,
input-history resimulation and native pose/height equivalence remain open.

Native sender1447696a0 copies an eligible controlling vehicle's current
StateVector into mPos and its completed velocity into mPosDelta. Position
subtraction is not that velocity. Preserve player-v7 unchanged and add a
separately negotiated revision-one vehicle frame paired to its same completed
frame identity. Ordinary Java and missing vehicle samples retain0020 fallback.

Bind optional vehicle physics to the tracked mount object, Java UUID/id,
native unique/runtime IDs, native actor lifetime and a connection-owned mount
epoch. Admit only primary direct root boat/horse controls outside spectator,
death, dimension, chunk and existing player position-sync waits. Do not infer
full native controlling-seat or ShouldBeSimulated equivalence from this gate.

Authority position changes invalidate pending mount physics and advance its
epoch even below matching tolerance. Local MOVE_VEHICLE coordinates retain
ordinary conversion without advancing authority epochs. Independent frame
watermarks survive consume/reject/correction; a frame cannot establish a
player input clock or overwrite valid pending physics with foreign identity.

Original instruction fixtures cover560 vehicle membership checks,22 sender
cases,six mount transitions,192 controlling-seat writers and24 callbacks.
Raw packet tests retain the velocity-versus-displacement negative control,
paired emission, identity/frame rejection, correction and transition gates.
Corrected vehicle velocity application, intent cleanup execution order,
complete native prediction/resimulation and live strict BDS/direct/ViaProxy
trajectories remain separate boundaries, with no production inference from
supplied native pool or StateVector inputs.

Publish mount context only after the actual native actor join and both PLAY
states. Re-registration republishes the same epoch; death/revival, mount,
capability, metadata and mode transitions cannot retain pre-transition pending
physics. Target horse callback14b17f0f0 requires raw flags8/28, matching the
protocol ActorFlags saddle/tame entries. Keep this narrow capability gate
separate from complete native ShouldBeSimulated/controlling-seat eligibility.

Withdraw the pending completed frame when its permission capability is
unregistered. Preserve frame watermark and correction history so an old
frame cannot replay after re-registration. Raw handler regressions cover
unregister, re-register and the next ordinary tick.

Use the native Player position anchor 1.62001F for both authoritative
teleports and completed-frame output. The target 1.26.51.1 constructor
1401dc620 initializes this value from PE address 14e6ae444. A private BDS
protocol 2193 ScriptAPI teleport to feet Y 70 sends player Y 71.62001037597656
(bits 428f3d72). Subtracting the previous 1.62F leaves Java feet Y
70.00000762939453; the native value restores 70 exactly. The recorded
Java completed frames independently expose that same drift.

A packet-level regression translates the captured native Y into exact
Java feet and returns four completed frames to the same native anchor.
This corrects the shared origin conversion; native aim selection, camera
update scheduling and complete physics trajectory parity remain separate.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 correction packet 161 dispatches through
1417342f0 and 14131ab90 to 144359100. Its original target selector and
replay lookup reject tick zero before command allocation. Preserve all
unsigned tick bits in the negotiated state, including negative Java longs.
The native lookup also compares the tick with the oldest retained clock.
Matching that cutoff requires actual snapshot history, still incomplete.
An 864-case instruction probe verifies these entry gates and full command
payload copying, with ECS, relation resolution, allocator and dispatch
boundaries. It does not establish command application or physics replay.

Apply the native vehicle correction velocity through Java entity motion,
including authoritative zero resets. MOVE_VEHICLE carries position and
rotation only. Java 26.3 applies entity motion to delta movement and its
vehicle position handler preserves that value. The original native command
144373f30 passes 48 controls for StateVector writes and conditional vehicle
rotation/angular writes. Typed store providers and the ground-tag setter
are fixture boundaries. Packet tests cover boat and horse delivery with
nonzero motion followed by zero. Historical replay and client ground or
angular reconciliation remain incomplete.

Configure emitted-input clock retention from START_GAME using the native
1..1000 history bound. Retain clocks without a completed prediction and
reset the ring on discontinuities, including duplicate or backward clocks,
while preserving contiguous unsigned wrap and the connection frame watermark.
Reject reserved zero and unsigned clocks older than the oldest retained
input before player or vehicle mutation, motion delivery or payload emission.
Do not impose an unverified future-tick upper bound.

Pinned 1.26.51.1 instructions execute 512 joined receiver, queue, comparator,
policy and replay controls with an explicit arithmetic physics witness.
Historical position commands compare against the next retained snapshot,
queue there and apply during replay rather than at receipt. Twelve original
installer/constructor controls establish the native ushort capacity clamp.
These probes do not establish malformed signed wire-size conversion or
native world physics. This change implements clock admission and retention;
actual historical component restoration and client physics replay remain open.

Retain local movement-effect input admissions and ordered server commands
through historical component replay. Snapshot complete effect values before
simulation, restore the first dirty frame, then apply queued unadjusted
commands, captured inputs and countdown. Refresh later snapshots during
replay so subsequent corrections retain already-corrected history.
Preserve zero-duration marker lifetime, pending command transfer and commands
with a zero movement mask until another command requests replay.

Transport the exact oldest retained unsigned input clock in movement-effect
revision 3. Clamp historical dispatch against that clock, including short
histories and clock discontinuities, instead of inventing a fixed window.
Do not accept revision 2 through a compatibility alias.

The pinned native receiver, complete capture caller, frame capture, input
fill and replay execute together in 128 controls. Four additional controls
verify that clock discontinuities discard old frames but retain pending
commands and movement masks. Original generated effect inputs reproduce
28 prior activity mismatches; production now matches all 336 immediate
controls and all 84 first historical replays. These controls do not establish
position restoration, native world physics or complete trajectory parity.

Verify the packed actor snapshot path before selecting mutable history
semantics. Original143276a40 and14327b6f0/14327b7c0 clone and restore
MovementEffectsComponent5c92321d at cached table slot72, including timestamps,
durations and kind. Eighteen controls execute those owned vector copies.
The earlier presence-only probe populated slot76, which is another type;
its claimed effect snapshot semantics were incorrect. The336 generated
input controls remain bounded by their supplied snapshot implementation.

Retain independent effect snapshots to prevent unrelated timers from aging
twice during a rewind. Refresh subsequent snapshots before commands and
input. Both regressions fail with the previous journal and pass with the
snapshot implementation. Sixty-four original correction/capture/replay
controls now use packed history comparison, capture and restore, including
StateVector36, AABBShape32, rotation16, angular20 and Offsets68. Ten original
VehicleComponent vector controls retain independent historical membership
and order. Controlled ECS/cache providers, extra restore-copy registration,
input phases and world physics remain separate boundaries and gaps.

Reject correction packets when no input clock is retained, matching the
original receiver before actor state can mutate. This includes initial
startup and START_GAME history reconfiguration. Future clocks remain
eligible for native command retention. Original receiver controls cover
128 player/vehicle cases, including 36 empty histories, and packet
regressions fail before the fix and pass afterward. Full physical actor
restoration and resimulation remain separate unfinished work.

Expose owned movement-effect values to the client physical replay adapter.
Keep the live prediction clock separate from historical effect reads. Apply
retained effect commands at their input frame and replace local admissions
when physical replay reevaluates sulfur contact.

Publish owned snapshots of native flow accumulation and cached Swift Sneak
input state. The add-on restores these values with its actor graph; the
core keeps command history and clock ownership.

Keep the completed frame watermark when a play listener is replaced on
one connection. Discard old actor values and queued commands without
making new prediction frames look like replays to the remote core.

Pending passenger links can resolve during actor registration, before the
spawn handler initializes vehicle coordinates. Keep prediction unavailable
until player and vehicle positions exist. The ordinary metadata update
then publishes the initialized mount context. This prevents the reproduced
BDS mount-spawn disconnect without granting authority to a partial actor.

Queue negotiated player and vehicle corrections against retained physical
frames instead of immediately changing the translated actor. Revision two
carries lifetime identity, exact velocity, optional boat angular velocity,
coordinate conventions and the emitted input-to-client-frame binding.
Keep Java position packets paired for their ordinary acknowledgments;
legacy connections retain direct position and motion handling.

The original 1.26.51.1 receiver at 0x144359100 supplies 128 clock controls:
reject zero and expired unsigned clocks, queue the following snapshot,
cap before incrementing, keep current-clock commands for the next capture,
and retain future commands without starting replay. Reproject the clock
when the client receives the correction, since it can have advanced since
core emitted the payload. Comparator 0x144373d90 uses float squared-vector
tolerance 0.00001 and optional angular tolerance 0.0001, with strict greater
comparison and separate float operations.

Preserve the current vehicle authority context while queuing a correction.
Discard unconsumed optional physics without manufacturing a new control
lease or losing the completed frame watermark. Validate exact typed
payloads and exercise paired player/boat/horse packets, full-precision
motion, unsigned wrap, delayed receipt, and the native comparator rules.
Historical world/collision inputs and full native pre/post-input phase
alignment remain separate requirements; fixture arithmetic does not
establish native trajectory parity.

Port the native collision replay input boundary rather than freezing a
whole historical world. CopyCollisionShapesRewindSystem at 0x148b73aa0
reuses captured shape/reference vectors and fetch bounds only when float
squared position distance is strictly below 4. The live gather kernel at
0x1431c2f50 caps motion at 16, builds step and lower-inset bounds, and queries
only uncovered cache regions. Keep native float plane traversal, 1e-5
advances, ordered deduplication and 0.001 volume merge comparisons.

Expose the used query/reuse calculations to the client physical input
adapter. Fresh execution controls compare 128 original gather cases and
64 original reuse cases with the Java implementation, including nonempty
prior vectors. Native world traversal, actor-reference lifetimes, nearby
solid refresh and complete movement trajectories remain separate from
these supplied-store and supplied-world-record instruction controls.

Port the native world visitor's X/Z/Y loaded-block order and float query
padding. Scan unavailable chunks in X-first order with eight-block margins
and preserve their full-height solid barriers in the retained geometry.
Reproduce the native floor below the world's minimum height.

The 1.26.51.1 visitor at 0x142d883c0 supplies this behavior. Production Java
traversal and null-source barriers match 64 fresh original instruction
controls. World, chunk state and block shapes are explicit fixture sources;
